.TH XUR 1 "2026-10-27" "xur 0.1.1" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur \- run and inspect Xur programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xur run
.RB [ \-\-no\-trace ]
.I file
.br
.B xur debug tokens
.I file
.br
.B xur debug ast
.I file
.br
.B xur debug resolve
.I file
.br
.B xur debug bytecode
.I file
.br
.B xur lsp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xur
is the command-line interface for the Xur programming language, a
dynamically-checked, expression-oriented language with Rust-like syntax.
Programs are compiled to bytecode and run on Xur's virtual machine.
.PP
A program's entry point is a
.B fn main()
function, which may optionally return
.BR Result<(),\ str> .
When
.B main
returns an
.BR Err ,
.B xur
prints the error message and exits with a non-zero status.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run \fIfile\fR
Compile and execute the program rooted at
.IR file .
Imports are resolved relative to the file's directory.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-trace
Suppress the chain of
.B ?
locations printed when an error propagates up to
.BR main .
.RE
.TP
.B debug tokens \fIfile\fR
Print the token stream produced by the lexer, one token per line, with
line:column, kind, and source text.
.TP
.B debug ast \fIfile\fR
Print the syntax tree produced by the parser.
.TP
.B debug resolve \fIfile\fR
Print what every name in the program resolves to after following imports.
.TP
.B debug bytecode \fIfile\fR
Print the compiled bytecode of every function in the program.
.TP
.B lsp
Start the language server, communicating over stdio.
Provides diagnostics, go-to-definition, hover, find-references,
completions, and parameter-name inlay hints.
Editors launch this as a subprocess;
see the Neovim and VS Code examples below.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xur run example/main.xur
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect a file's tokens:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xur debug tokens example/main.xur
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran successfully (or
.B main
returned
.BR Ok ).
.TP
.B 1
A compile error, runtime error, or
.B Err
from
.BR main .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7),
.BR xur.std.re (7)
